### BookVerse — Lesson 03 experiments: partial failure & idempotency
### Requires the "REST Client" VS Code extension. Run top to bottom.
###
### IMPORTANT: idempotency keys are permanent. Once a key is used it stays used,
### so re-running this file with the same keys replays instead of creating.
### Bump @run before each fresh pass.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
@email = amr@example.com
@password = password123 

### ⬇⬇ BUMP THIS BEFORE EACH FULL RE-RUN ⬇⬇
@run = 1

@keyReplay   = replay-{{run}}
@keyDistinctA = distinct-a-{{run}}
@keyDistinctB = distinct-b-{{run}}
@keyTimeout  = timeout-{{run}}
@keyBugFirst = clientbug-first-{{run}}
@keyBugRetry = clientbug-retry-{{run}}

### ============================================================
### SETUP
### ============================================================

### S1. Gateway health
GET {{baseUrl}}/health

### S2. Login — capture the access token
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Grab the token out of the login response (needs its own ### block —
### a bare @var line after a body is parsed AS part of that body)
@accessToken = {{login.response.body.data.tokens.accessToken}}

### S3. Baseline — how many books exist right now?
GET {{baseUrl}}/books


### ============================================================
### TEST 1 — Replay: same key, same body, twice
### EXPECT: 201 both times, IDENTICAL book id. Only one book created.
### ============================================================

### 1a. First create
# @name t1a
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{keyReplay}}

{
  "title": "T1 Replay Probe",
  "description": "same key, same body",
  "genre": ["test"]
}

### 1b. Send it again, unchanged. → 201 with the SAME id as 1a
###     Compare against: {{t1a.response.body.data.id}}
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{keyReplay}}

{
  "title": "T1 Replay Probe",
  "description": "same key, same body",
  "genre": ["test"]
}


### ============================================================
### TEST 2 — Fingerprint mismatch: same key, DIFFERENT body
### EXPECT: 409 Conflict. The client reused a key for a new intent.
### ============================================================

### 2a. Reuse keyReplay with a different title
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{keyReplay}}

{
  "title": "T2 Different Title",
  "description": "same key, same body",
  "genre": ["test"]
}

### 2b. Same key, same fields, DIFFERENT key ORDER in the JSON.
###     EXPECT: 201 replay, not 409 — the hash sorts keys before hashing.
###     If this 409s, canonicalization is broken.
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{keyReplay}}

{
  "genre": ["test"],
  "description": "same key, same body",
  "title": "T1 Replay Probe"
}


### ============================================================
### TEST 3 — Different keys, same body → TWO books
### EXPECT: two 201s with DIFFERENT ids. Catches over-aggressive dedupe:
### a user may legitimately create two identical books.
### ============================================================

### 3a.
# @name t3a
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{keyDistinctA}}

{
  "title": "T3 Duplicate Allowed",
  "description": "different keys, same body",
  "genre": ["test"]
}

### 3b. Different key, identical body. → 201, id DIFFERENT from {{t3a.response.body.data.id}}
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{keyDistinctB}}

{
  "title": "T3 Duplicate Allowed",
  "description": "different keys, same body",
  "genre": ["test"]
}


### ============================================================
### TEST 4 — THE ONE THAT MATTERS
### Gateway times out (1s) while book-service keeps working (8s fault).
### The client retries with the SAME key.
### EXPECT: 4a → 504.  4b → 201 with the book 4a created.  ONE book total.
### ============================================================

### 4a. Fault-inject 8s. Gateway gives up at 1s → 504.
###     The write still commits ~7s later. You were told it failed. It didn't.
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{keyTimeout}}
x-fault-delay: 8000

{
  "title": "T4 Timeout Then Retry",
  "description": "the payoff",
  "genre": ["test"]
}

### 4b. >>> WAIT ~10 SECONDS <<< then retry with the same key, no fault.
###     EXPECT 201 and a book id — the one 4a silently created.
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{keyTimeout}}

{
  "title": "T4 Timeout Then Retry",
  "description": "the payoff",
  "genre": ["test"]
}

### 4c. Count them. EXPECT exactly ONE "T4 Timeout Then Retry".
GET {{baseUrl}}/books


### ============================================================
### TEST 5 — The client bug this protects against
### A client that generates a NEW key per HTTP request instead of per
### INTENT gets no protection at all.
### EXPECT: 5a → 504, 5b → 201, and TWO books. The duplicate is back.
### ============================================================

### 5a. Fault-inject, times out.
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{keyBugFirst}}
x-fault-delay: 8000

{
  "title": "T5 Duplicate Returns",
  "description": "new key per retry",
  "genre": ["test"]
}

### 5b. >>> WAIT ~10 SECONDS <<< retry, but with a FRESH key (the bug).
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{keyBugRetry}}

{
  "title": "T5 Duplicate Returns",
  "description": "new key per retry",
  "genre": ["test"]
}

### 5c. EXPECT TWO "T5 Duplicate Returns". The key must identify the
###     INTENT, not the request.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books


### ============================================================
### TEST 6 — Missing key
### EXPECT: 400. You made the key required; this is that decision.
### ============================================================

POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "T6 No Key",
  "description": "should be rejected",
  "genre": ["test"]
}


